{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Discovering Blender Python Operators.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Discovering Blender Python Operators.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"discovering blender python operators discovering blender python operators","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Discovering Blender Python Operators.md","fileName":"Discovering Blender Python Operators.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Discovering Blender Python Operators","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 09:04\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Blender Python Scripting Environment]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Blender Python Scripting Environment","Blender Command Log","blenderpythonapi.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Discovering Blender Python Operators.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Discovering Blender Python Operators.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"discovering blender python operators discovering blender python operators","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Discovering Blender Python Operators.md","fileName":"Discovering Blender Python Operators.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Discovering Blender Python Operators","heading":"Discovering Blender Python Operators","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Blender makes operator discovery an empirical process. A developer can perform the desired interface action, inspect the resulting call in the [[Blender Command Log]], and use Python tooltips or console autocompletion to inspect the operator's parameters.\n\nThe discovered call should then be tested in the current mode and selection state because many operators depend on `bpy.context`. This method turns ordinary interface use into API exploration while also revealing which actions map cleanly to `bpy.ops` and which require direct work with Blender data.","tags